# Citation File Format 1.2.0. https://citation-file-format.github.io/
#
# This file describes the CODE in this repository. The released tables are a separate deposit
# with its own DOI, and that deposit is what most users should cite; `preferred-citation` below
# carries it, so GitHub's "Cite this repository" button and any tool that reads CFF will offer
# the dataset rather than the pipeline.
#
# There is deliberately no .zenodo.json beside this file. Where both are present Zenodo reads
# only .zenodo.json and ignores this file entirely
# (https://help.zenodo.org/docs/github/describe-software/).
#
# `doi` and `date-released` for the software are absent on purpose. Both are known only once a
# GitHub Release has been archived by Zenodo. Add the VERSION doi at that point, not the concept
# doi: the tables a given run produces are the output of one state of this pipeline, so a
# citation that does not name the version has not said which code built the numbers.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use the data, cite the openICPSR deposit under `preferred-citation`. If you use or modify this pipeline, cite the software entry below and name the version you ran."
type: software
title: "KIRD build pipeline"
abstract: >-
  Build and analysis code for KIRD, a harmonized district-level panel of foreign residents in
  South Korea, 2006-2025. The Ministry of Justice and the Ministry of the Interior and Safety
  publish each year as a separate workbook whose layout, nationality names, visa codes, and
  district names change between editions, so the same district in two different years joins on
  nothing. This pipeline reconciles the naming across editions, carries administrative boundary
  changes through the panel, and computes every derived measure on one basis for all years:
  foreign share of population, nationality diversity, residential segregation against Korean
  nationals, ethnic-enclave classification, and the number of speakers of each language implied
  by the nationality composition. It emits the 17 tidy tables released in the deposit, in CSV
  and labeled Stata format, alongside a bilingual data dictionary.
authors:
  - family-names: Yoo
    given-names: Nari
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8467-7048"
    email: nariyoo@umich.edu
    affiliation: University of Michigan School of Social Work
    website: "https://nariyoo.com"
version: 1.1.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/nariyoo/kird-korea-immigration"
license: MIT
license-url: "https://github.com/nariyoo/kird-korea-immigration/blob/main/LICENSE"
keywords:
  - immigration
  - South Korea
  - administrative data
  - residential segregation
  - nationality diversity
  - language demand
  - data harmonization
  - reproducible pipeline

preferred-citation:
  type: data
  title: "Spatiotemporal administrative dataset of nationality, residential diversity, and language demand among immigrants in South Korea, 2006–2024"
  authors:
